TI  - Cell-free placental DNA: What do we really know?
PG  - e1011484
LID - 10.1371/journal.pgen.1011484 [doi]
LID - e1011484
AB  - Cell-free placental DNA (cfpDNA) is present in maternal circulation during 
      gestation. CfpDNA carries great potential as a research and clinical tool as it 
      provides a means to investigate the placental (epi)genome across gestation, which 
      previously required invasive placenta sampling procedures. CfpDNA has been widely 
      implemented in the clinical setting for noninvasive prenatal testing (NIPT). 
      Despite this, the basic biology of cfpDNA remains poorly understood, limiting the 
      research and clinical utility of cfpDNA. This review will examine the current 
      knowledge of cfpDNA, including origins and molecular characteristics, highlight 
      gaps in knowledge, and discuss future research directions.
